---
import ModuleLayout from '../../layouts/ModuleLayout.astro';
import modules from '../../data/modules.json';
import '../../styles/modules/literature.css';
import '../../styles/icons/literature.css';

const mod = modules.find((m) => m.id === 31)!;
---

<ModuleLayout mod={mod}>

  

    <div class="module-header">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-book-open module-icon-large"></i>
      <div>
        <div class="deep-dive-label">Module 31 — Deep Dive</div>
        <h1 class="module-title module-heading">Literature</h1>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="module-subtitle">Language, narrative, and the written word as art form</p>


    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART A · WHAT LITERATURE IS
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="part-section" id="part-0">

      <h2 class="sec" id="part-a-what-literature-is-the-foundations">Part A · what literature is — the foundations</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">Language used as art</p>
        <p class="cval">The art form made of the medium of thought itself</p>
        <p class="cnote">Literature is language organised beyond its purely informational function — shaped for aesthetic effect, emotional resonance, and the creation of worlds. It is the only art form made of the same material as human thought. A painting or a symphony is experienced through the senses; literature works through language, which is already structured by concepts, grammar, and meaning. This makes it simultaneously the most intimate art form (it happens inside the reader's mind) and the most culturally contingent (it requires deep knowledge of a specific language). Homer's Iliad has been read for 2,800 years not because it provides information but because it tells the truth about war, glory, grief, and mortality in ways that transcend its original context.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Four literary properties -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">What makes writing "literary"? — the key properties</div>
        <div class="literature-grid">
          <div class="literature-panel">
            <div class="literature-label">Density of language</div>
            <div class="trait-desc-density">Literary writing earns re-reading. Each sentence is chosen, not merely adequate. Poetry maximises this: the poem is untranslatable because every word, sound, and position contributes. A news article communicates its content; a poem is its form.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="trait-panel-ambiguity">
            <div class="trait-title-ambiguity">Ambiguity and depth</div>
            <div class="trait-desc-ambiguity">Literary language carries multiple meanings simultaneously. "To be or not to be" is not a question that has a single correct answer. The ambiguity is the point. Richness of interpretation is a sign of quality, not of vagueness. The text doesn't tell you what to think; it gives you something to think with.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="trait-panel-form">
            <div class="trait-title-form">Attention to form</div>
            <div class="trait-desc-form">Literature is conscious of how it says things, not just what. The shape of a sonnet (14 lines, iambic pentameter, a volta, a couplet) creates a specific pressure that shapes meaning. The way Faulkner's sentences sprawl and double back enacts the consciousness they describe.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="trait-panel-durability">
            <div class="trait-title-durability">Cultural durability</div>
            <div class="trait-desc-durability">Literary works accrue meaning through time. Hamlet means something different in 1601, 1900, and 2025 — each era finds new resonances. This accretion of interpretation distinguishes literature from entertainment, which is consumed and discarded. The canon is, in part, a list of works that have survived this test.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Genre explorer -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">The major literary genres — click to explore</div>
        <div class="genre-grid">
          <div class="genre-card active" onclick="showGenre('poetry', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">📜</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Poetry</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">The oldest and densest</div>
          </div>
          <div class="genre-card" onclick="showGenre('novel', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">📗</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Novel</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">The modern form</div>
          </div>
          <div class="genre-card" onclick="showGenre('shortstory', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">📄</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Short story</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">Concentrated prose</div>
          </div>
          <div class="genre-card" onclick="showGenre('drama', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">🎭</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Drama</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">Writing for performance</div>
          </div>
          <div class="genre-card" onclick="showGenre('essay', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">✍️</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Essay</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">Thought made visible</div>
          </div>
          <div class="genre-card" onclick="showGenre('epic', this)">
            <div class="card-icon">⚔️</div>
            <div class="label-strong">Epic</div>
            <div class="card-sub-muted">The founding form</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="genre-display" class="genre-detail show"></div>

      <!-- Timeline: forms of literature across history -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Literary forms across time — approximate dominance</div>
        <div class="mini-intro">Each era is dominated by different forms. These are rough centres of gravity, not rigid boundaries.</div>
        <div class="pc-axis">
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 0%">–800 BCE</span>
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 20%">500 CE</span>
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 45%">1500</span>
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 65%">1700</span>
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 80%">1850</span>
          <span class="axis-tick-small axis-tick-small-data" style="--data-left: 100%">Now</span>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Oral epic &amp; lyric poetry</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: var(--accent-bg); --data-left: 0%; --data-width: 30%">
              <span class="period-label text-indigo-900">Homer, Sappho, Virgil</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Drama (stage)</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: var(--color-background-warning); --data-left: 5%; --data-width: 55%">
              <span class="period-label text-warning">Sophocles → Shakespeare → Ibsen</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Lyric &amp; narrative poetry</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: var(--color-background-info); --data-left: 0%; --data-width: 80%">
              <span class="period-label text-info">Dominant through Romantic era</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Novel</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: var(--color-background-success); --data-left: 58%; --data-width: 42%">
              <span class="period-label text-success">Defoe, Austen → Present</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Personal essay</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: #FAECE7; --data-left: 40%; --data-width: 60%">
              <span class="period-label text-orange-900">Montaigne (1580) → Now</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-row-loose">
          <div class="pc-label">Short story</div>
          <div class="period-track">
            <div class="pc-fill pc-fill-data" style="--data-background: var(--color-background-danger); --data-left: 68%; --data-width: 32%">
              <span class="period-label text-danger">Poe, Chekhov → Now</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Distribution bar -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Where readers spend their time — approximate distribution of literary reading</div>
        <div class="stacked-bar-compact">
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: #8A6DB5; --data-width: 52%"><span class="seg-label">52% Novels</span></div>
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: var(--accent); --data-width: 15%"><span class="seg-label">15% SS</span></div>
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: #E07B5A; --data-width: 12%"><span class="seg-label">12% NF</span></div>
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: #5DCAA5; --data-width: 10%"><span class="seg-label">10% Drama</span></div>
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: #EF9F27; --data-width: 7%"><span class="seg-label-small">7% Poetry</span></div>
          <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-data" style="--data-background: #AFA9EC; --data-width: 4%"><span class="seg-label-small">4%+</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-list">
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot dist-leg-dot-style"></div>Novel</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot bg-accent"></div>Short story</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot dist-leg-dot-coral"></div>Literary non-fiction</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot dist-leg-dot-teal"></div>Drama / Play</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot bg-amber-500"></div>Poetry</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><div class="key-dot dist-leg-dot-lavender"></div>Other (essay, etc.)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="muted-text-small">Rough indicative estimates based on sales and reading surveys. Poetry's small share understates its cultural presence — most people who don't "read poetry" quote it constantly without knowing.</div>
      </div>


    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART B · POETRY AND VERSE
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    </div><!-- /part-0 -->
    <div class="part-section" id="part-1">

      <h2 class="sec" id="part-b-poetry-and-verse-how-poems-make-meaning">Part B · poetry &amp; verse — how poems make meaning</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">Language at maximum compression</p>
        <p class="cval">Poetry is not prose with line breaks — it is a different kind of object</p>
        <p class="cnote">The defining feature of poetry is the line, not the sentence. The line break creates a pressure the sentence doesn't have: it forces a pause, an emphasis, a double reading. When Keats writes "Beauty is truth, truth beauty" he is exploiting the reversibility of a sentence structure that prose rarely uses. When William Carlos Williams writes "so much depends / upon / a red wheel / barrow" he is using the line break to make each fragment carry weight. Poetry is the art form that most insists on the inseparability of form and content — and this is why it cannot be paraphrased without loss. A poem about grief is not information about grief; it is a particular experience of grief made available to the reader through specific language choices.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Metre visualiser -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="inline-card-title">Metre in English poetry — hear the beat</div>
        <div class="mini-intro-spaced">Stressed syllables are highlighted. Iambic pentameter — the metre of Shakespeare and Milton — has 5 iambs (unstressed-stressed) per line: 10 syllables, naturally close to spoken English rhythm.</div>
        <div class="u-mb-md">
          <div class="poem-citation">Shakespeare, Sonnet 18 — iambic pentameter (5 iambs per line)</div>
          <div class="metre-line">
            <span class="syl syl-u">Shall</span><span class="syl syl-s">I</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">com</span><span class="syl syl-s">-pare</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">thee</span><span class="syl syl-s">to</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">a</span><span class="syl syl-s">sum</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">-mer's</span><span class="syl syl-s">day?</span>
            <span class="metre-label">iambic pentameter</span>
          </div>
          <div class="metre-line">
            <span class="syl syl-u">Thou</span><span class="syl syl-s">art</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">more</span><span class="syl syl-s">love</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">-ly</span><span class="syl syl-s">and</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">more</span><span class="syl syl-s">tem</span>
            <span class="syl syl-u">-pe</span><span class="syl syl-s">-rate</span>
            <span class="metre-label">iambic pentameter</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="u-mb-md">
          <div class="poem-citation">Longfellow, Song of Hiawatha — trochaic octameter (8 trochees: stressed-unstressed)</div>
          <div class="metre-line">
            <span class="syl syl-s">By</span><span class="syl syl-u">the</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">shores</span><span class="syl syl-u">of</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">Gi</span><span class="syl syl-u">-tche</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">Gu</span><span class="syl syl-u">-mee</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">By</span><span class="syl syl-u">the</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">shi</span><span class="syl syl-u">-ning</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">Big</span><span class="syl syl-u">-Sea</span>
            <span class="syl syl-s">Wa</span><span class="syl syl-u">-ter</span>
            <span class="metre-label">trochaic octameter</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="muted-text-small">Metre is not a cage — it is a tension. Free verse (Whitman, most 20th-century poetry) abandons fixed metre but creates rhythm through repetition, breath, and syntax. The ear still expects patterns; free verse creates meaning by meeting and frustrating those expectations.</div>
      </div>

      <!-- Poetic forms explorer (multi-card) -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="poetic-forms-the-containers-of-verse">poetic forms — the containers of verse</h2>
      <div class="lede-text-loose">Form is not decoration. The sonnet's 14-line constraint forces a compression that shapes thought. The haiku's 17-syllable limit demands that one image contain a world. Click each form to see how it works.</div>
      <div class="elem-grid" id="form-grid"></div>
      <div class="elem-detail elem-detail-panel" id="form-detail"></div>

      <!-- Poetic devices explorer panel -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="poetic-devices-the-tools-of-the-trade">poetic devices — the tools of the trade</h2>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Key devices — click to see how each one works</div>
        <div class="button-row-compact" id="device-btns"></div>
        <div class="explorer-output" id="device-display"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Comparison scale: poetry traditions -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="poetry-traditions-a-comparative-map">poetry traditions — a comparative map</h2>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="compact-card-title">Major poets — formal vs free verse tendency</div>
        <div class="block-gap">
          <div class="cscale-poles">
            <span>Strictly formal (fixed metre &amp; rhyme)</span>
            <span>Pure free verse (no fixed metre)</span>
          </div>
          <div id="poet-scale"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="muted-text-small">Position indicates general tendency across their body of work — many poets (like Yeats) moved significantly over their careers. The scale reflects primary practice, not occasional experiments.</div>
      </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART C · THE NOVEL
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <!-- Bar chart: poetry reading stats -->
      <div class="block-gap content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Most-assigned poets in English literature courses (approximate relative frequency)</div>
        <div id="poet-bars"></div>
        <div class="caption-spaced">Based on syllabi analysis from US and UK universities. Shakespeare's sonnets, though technically poetry, are often taught in drama courses and undercounted here.</div>
      </div>

    </div><!-- /part-1 -->
    <div class="part-section" id="part-2">

      <h2 class="sec" id="part-c-the-novel-the-dominant-art-form-of-modernity">Part C · the novel — the dominant art form of modernity</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">The novel defined</p>
        <p class="cval">Extended prose fiction — typically 60,000 to 200,000 words — that creates an immersive world through sustained narrative</p>
        <p class="cnote">The novel emerged in the 18th century as the art form of the modern individual. Its subject is the interior life of specific people in social worlds. Cervantes's Don Quixote (1605) is often named the first novel; Daniel Defoe's Robinson Crusoe (1719) established the naturalistic template. By the 19th century the novel had become the dominant literary form in Western culture, absorbing the social documentary function of the essay, the emotional register of poetry, and the dramatic tension of theatre. Its defining technical achievement is free indirect discourse: the ability to inhabit a character's consciousness without formally switching to first-person narration. "She was tired of it all" — whose thought is this? The narrator's? Elizabeth Bennet's? Jane Austen discovered that the ambiguity is the point.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Novel history timeline -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="inline-card-title">The novel in time — major phases</div>
        <div class="mini-intro">Each phase produced a distinct set of formal techniques and concerns.</div>
        <div id="novel-timeline"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Narrative elements multi-card -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="narrative-elements-the-building-blocks-of-fiction">narrative elements — the building blocks of fiction</h2>
      <div class="lede-text-loose">Every novel deploys these elements. The craft is in their combination — and in the choices the novelist makes about which to foreground and which to suppress.</div>
      <div class="elem-grid" id="narr-grid"></div>
      <div class="elem-detail elem-detail-panel" id="narr-detail"></div>

      <!-- National traditions explorer -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="national-traditions-how-culture-shapes-the-novel">national traditions — how culture shapes the novel</h2>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Major novelistic traditions — select a tradition</div>
        <div class="button-row-compact" id="trad-btns"></div>
        <div class="explorer-output" id="trad-display"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Slider: narrative distance -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="inline-card-title">Narrative distance — the spectrum from intimate to remote</div>
        <div class="mini-intro">How close is the narrator to the character's consciousness? Slide to explore the full range.</div>
        <div class="axis-ends-tight">
          <span>Deep inside a character's mind</span>
          <span>Distant, god-like narrator</span>
        </div>
        <input class="u-w-100pct" type="range" id="u-w-100pct" min="1" max="5" value="3" oninput="updateDistanceSlider(this.value)">
        <div class="slider-output" id="distance-output"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- SVG: anatomy of a chapter -->
      <div class="scroll-panel">
        <div class="block-title">Anatomy of a chapter — the narrative cycle</div>
        <svg class="literature-media" viewBox="0 0 700 240" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arrowhead" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="7" refX="9" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
              <polygon points="0 0, 10 3.5, 0 7" fill="#8A6DB5" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <!-- Boxes -->
          <rect x="10" y="95" width="110" height="50" rx="8" fill="#EEEDFE" stroke="#AFA9EC" stroke-width="0.8"/>
          <text x="65" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#534AB7">Scene setting</text>
          <text x="65" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#534AB7">place, time, mood</text>

          <rect x="155" y="95" width="110" height="50" rx="8" fill="#E6F1FB" stroke="#85B7EB" stroke-width="0.8"/>
          <text x="210" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#0C447C">Conflict / desire</text>
          <text x="210" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#0C447C">character wants something</text>

          <rect x="300" y="95" width="110" height="50" rx="8" fill="#FAEEDA" stroke="#EF9F27" stroke-width="0.8"/>
          <text x="355" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#633806">Complication</text>
          <text x="355" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#633806">obstacle / revelation</text>

          <rect x="445" y="95" width="110" height="50" rx="8" fill="#EAF3DE" stroke="#97C459" stroke-width="0.8"/>
          <text x="500" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#27500A">Decision / action</text>
          <text x="500" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#27500A">character acts or fails</text>

          <rect x="590" y="95" width="100" height="50" rx="8" fill="#FAECE7" stroke="#E07B5A" stroke-width="0.8"/>
          <text x="640" y="116" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#712B13">New situation</text>
          <text x="640" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#712B13">world is changed</text>

          <!-- Arrows -->
          <line x1="120" y1="120" x2="153" y2="120" stroke="#8A6DB5" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/>
          <line x1="265" y1="120" x2="298" y2="120" stroke="#8A6DB5" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/>
          <line x1="410" y1="120" x2="443" y2="120" stroke="#8A6DB5" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/>
          <line x1="555" y1="120" x2="588" y2="120" stroke="#8A6DB5" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/>

          <!-- Arc for feedback loop -->
          <path d="M 640 145 Q 640 200 355 200 Q 65 200 65 145" fill="none" stroke="#AFA9EC" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4,3" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/>
          <text x="350" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">Each chapter advances the situation — creates the next chapter's conditions</text>

          <!-- Labels above -->
          <text x="65" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">1</text>
          <text x="210" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">2</text>
          <text x="355" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">3</text>
          <text x="500" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">4</text>
          <text x="640" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">5</text>
        </svg>
      </div>

      <!-- Reading time calculator -->
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="panel-title">Reading time calculator — how long will a novel take?</div>
        <div class="control-row-tight">
          <span class="calc-label">Novel word count (words)</span>
          <input class="u-w-130px" type="number" id="u-w-130px" value="100000" min="10000" max="800000">
        </div>
        <div class="control-row-tight">
          <span class="calc-label">Your reading speed (WPM)</span>
          <input class="u-w-130px" type="number" id="read-speed" value="250" min="50" max="700">
          <select class="u-fs-12px" id="u-fs-12px" onchange="applySpeedPreset(this.value)">
            <option value="">Preset...</option>
            <option value="150">Slow &amp; careful (150)</option>
            <option value="250">Average adult (250)</option>
            <option value="350">Practised reader (350)</option>
            <option value="500">Speed reader (500)</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="control-row-tight">
          <span class="calc-label">Minutes reading per day</span>
          <input class="u-w-130px" type="number" id="read-mins" value="30" min="5" max="480">
        </div>
        <button onclick="calcReadingTime()">Calculate</button>
        <div class="calc-result" id="reading-result"></div>
      </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART D · DRAMA
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    </div><!-- /part-2 -->
    <div class="part-section" id="part-3">

      <h2 class="sec" id="part-d-drama-writing-for-bodies-in-space">Part D · drama — writing for bodies in space</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">The most social of literary forms</p>
        <p class="cval">Drama is writing designed for performance before a live audience — it requires collaboration and communal reception</p>
        <p class="cnote">Drama cannot use the novelist's most powerful tool: interior monologue. Everything must be externalised through action, dialogue, and physical presence. A novelist can write "she was furious"; a playwright must show it — in what she says, in what she refuses to say, in what she does with her hands. This severe constraint is also drama's special power. It forces a precision that no other form requires. The live performance adds an irreversible present tense: Hamlet's "To be or not to be" is happening now, before you, and it will never be exactly this performance again. Drama is the art form that most insists on the body — and on the social fact of shared attention.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Greek tragedy structure SVG -->
      <div class="scroll-panel">
        <div class="block-title">Greek tragedy structure — the five-act arc</div>
        <svg class="literature-media" viewBox="0 0 700 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <!-- Background line -->
          <line x1="30" y1="160" x2="670" y2="160" stroke="#AFA9EC" stroke-width="1"/>

          <!-- Arc of fortune (protagonist rises then falls) -->
          <path d="M 60 150 Q 200 30 350 60 Q 480 85 640 155" fill="none" stroke="#8A6DB5" stroke-width="2.5"/>

          <!-- Labels at stages -->
          <circle cx="60" cy="150" r="6" fill="#8A6DB5"/>
          <text x="60" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="600" fill="#534AB7">Exposition</text>
          <text x="60" y="192" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">situation introduced</text>

          <circle cx="190" cy="65" r="6" fill="#8A6DB5"/>
          <text x="190" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="600" fill="#534AB7">Rising action</text>
          <text x="190" y="57" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">complication builds</text>

          <circle cx="350" cy="55" r="8" fill="#E07B5A"/>
          <text x="350" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#712B13">Crisis / Climax</text>
          <text x="350" y="47" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">point of no return</text>

          <circle cx="500" cy="95" r="6" fill="#8A6DB5"/>
          <text x="500" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="600" fill="#534AB7">Falling action</text>
          <text x="500" y="192" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">consequences unfold</text>

          <circle cx="640" cy="150" r="6" fill="#8A6DB5"/>
          <text x="640" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="600" fill="#534AB7">Catastrophe</text>
          <text x="640" y="192" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#888780">hero's destruction</text>

          <!-- Hamartia label -->
          <text x="350" y="15" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#E07B5A" font-style="italic">hamartia (fatal flaw) leads here</text>
          <text x="350" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#E07B5A" font-style="italic">catharsis felt by audience throughout</text>
        </svg>
        <div class="caption-spaced">Aristotle identified hamartia (a fatal error or character flaw), peripeteia (sudden reversal of fortune), anagnorisis (recognition of the truth), and catharsis (the emotional purging experienced by the audience) as the key mechanisms of tragedy in his Poetics (c. 335 BCE). All four are present in Sophocles's Oedipus Rex — still the clearest illustration of each concept.</div>
      </div>

      <!-- Playwright explorer panel -->
      <h2 class="sec" id="key-playwrights-the-dramatists-who-changed-the-form">key playwrights — the dramatists who changed the form</h2>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Select a playwright to explore their contribution</div>
        <div class="button-row-compact" id="playwright-btns"></div>
        <div class="explorer-output" id="playwright-display"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Drama types comparison scale -->
      <div class="content-panel">
        <div class="compact-card-title">Major plays — realism vs stylisation spectrum</div>
        <div class="block-gap">
          <div class="cscale-poles">
            <span>Naturalistic realism (slice of life)</span>
            <span>Highly stylised / abstract</span>
          </div>
