    <div class="part-section" id="part-4">

      <h2 class="sec" id="part-e-literary-movements-how-literature-changes-itself">Part E · literary movements — how literature changes itself</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">Movements as arguments</p>
        <p class="cval">Every literary movement is a reaction — an argument about what literature should be doing that the previous generation was failing to do</p>
        <p class="cnote">Romanticism reacted against Enlightenment rationalism. Realism reacted against Romantic idealism. Naturalism pushed Realism further toward biology and determinism. Modernism reacted against 19th-century narrative conventions. Postmodernism reacted against Modernism's earnestness about meaning. Understanding each movement requires understanding what it was against — and what problem it was trying to solve. Movements are never monolithic: the writers grouped together under a label often disagreed violently with each other, and the most interesting writers (Henry James, Dostoevsky, Kafka) resist categorisation entirely. Labels are maps, not territories.</p>
      </div>

