      <h2 class="sec" id="part-f-the-canon-what-it-is-and-why-it-matters-and-who-it-excludes">Part F · the canon — what it is and why it matters and who it excludes</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">The Western literary canon</p>
        <p class="cval">The informal list of works considered essential to literary education — and one of the most contested ideas in contemporary culture</p>
        <p class="cnote">The "canon" (from the Greek for measuring rod or standard) is not a fixed list. No authoritative body issues it. It is a collective judgment, accumulated over centuries through university syllabi, critical editions, translation history, and cultural reproduction. Harold Bloom's The Western Canon (1994) named 26 authors as central; F.R. Leavis's The Great Tradition (1948) named 4 novelists. The canon's defenders argue that it preserves genuinely great works from the erosion of time. Its critics — feminist, postcolonial, and Marxist — argue that it reflects the preferences of dead white European men and systematically excludes women, non-Western writers, and working-class voices. Both arguments are substantially correct, which makes the canon question one of the most productive debates in contemporary literary culture.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Canon formation explorer -->
