      <h2 class="sec" id="part-g-how-to-read-well-techniques-for-deeper-engagement">Part G · how to read well — techniques for deeper engagement</h2>

      <div class="card u-mb-lg">
        <p class="clabel">Reading is a skill</p>
        <p class="cval">Close reading — attending to the precise choices a writer makes — is the foundation of literary understanding</p>
        <p class="cnote">Most reading is passive: we absorb the meaning and move on. Literary reading is active: we attend to the how as much as the what, noticing word choice, sentence rhythm, structural patterns, and what is left unsaid. This is close reading — the method developed in the 20th century by the New Critics (I.A. Richards, F.R. Leavis, William Empson) as the basis of academic literary study. Empson's Seven Types of Ambiguity (1930) demonstrated that attending closely to a few words in a poem could open an entire world of competing meanings. The method is not just academic: it trains the general capacity to read any text carefully — legal documents, political speeches, advertisements — and to hear what they are doing as well as what they are saying.</p>
      </div>

