    <h2 class="sec" id="part-i-q-and-a">Part I · Q&A</h2>

      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">1. Is literary criticism just subjective opinion?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">No — though it's not purely objective either. Literary criticism occupies a middle space: it makes claims that can be supported by evidence from the text, argued, challenged, and refined. "Hamlet is about indecision" is not a fact, but it's not simply an opinion either — it's a claim with textual support that can be made more or less convincingly. The discipline trains readers to make better arguments about literary meaning, not to discover pre-existing correct interpretations.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">2. Why does literature matter when we can just read history for facts?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Literature doesn't compete with history on factual grounds — it does something different. It gives you access to the interior of another consciousness: what it felt like to be a 19th-century Russian serf (Chekhov), to live through a war (Remarque, Hemingway, Tolstoy), to be a woman in a patriarchal society (Austen, Woolf, Plath). Martha Nussbaum argues that literature cultivates moral imagination — the ability to inhabit another perspective — which is constitutive of ethical reasoning, not supplementary to it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">3. What's the difference between literary fiction and genre fiction?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Genre fiction (mystery, thriller, romance, science fiction, fantasy) foregrounds plot and formula — the reader knows the basic shape in advance and is satisfied when it's delivered. Literary fiction foregrounds language, character, and ambiguity — it resists formula and leaves questions open. But the distinction is not a quality judgment: the best genre fiction transcends the category (Dostoevsky writes thrillers; Kafka writes science fiction; Austen writes romance). And a lot of self-styled literary fiction is as formulaic as the genre it disdains.</div>
      </div>



      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">4. Why do we need metre? Doesn't it just make poetry sound artificial?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Metre creates expectations that can be met or broken — and both are meaningful. When Shakespeare writes iambic pentameter and then inserts a stressed syllable where an unstressed one is expected, the irregularity signals something: an emotional peak, a character's agitation, a thematic emphasis. The constraint is the instrument. Think of it like musical time signature: the beat gives the syncopation its meaning. Free verse doesn't abolish rhythm; it borrows the reader's expectation of rhythm and then creates meaning through variation against it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">5. Is it legitimate to interpret a poem differently from what the poet intended?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Yes — and for a principled reason. Poems are not transcriptions of the poet's intention; they are linguistic objects that generate meaning through the interaction of words, and that meaning is not controlled by the poet once the poem is published. Keats did not intend all the interpretations subsequent critics have made of his odes, but those interpretations are not wrong merely because he didn't anticipate them. What matters is whether an interpretation is supported by the text. W.K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley called the fallacy of excessive reliance on authorial intent "the intentional fallacy" (1946) — a useful corrective, even if their position was overstated.</div>
      </div>


      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">6. Why is free indirect discourse so important to the novel?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Free indirect discourse lets the narrator inhabit a character's consciousness without formally entering first-person. In "It was a truth universally acknowledged..." Austen both states a social cliche and ironises it through the deadpan third-person framing — you hear Elizabeth Bennet's scepticism in the narrator's voice. This technique gives novelists the best of both worlds: the intimacy of first-person combined with the authorial control of third-person. Henry James, Flaubert, and Woolf developed it to extraordinary sophistication. Without it, the psychological novel as we know it would not exist.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">7. Are long novels inherently better than short ones?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">No — and confusing length with ambition is a persistent mistake. Kafka's The Metamorphosis is 22,000 words and explores alienation, family, and transformation more richly than many 600-page novels. The novella form (Melville's Billy Budd, Conrad's Heart of Darkness, Henry James's The Turn of the Screw) is arguably the most perfect prose form — long enough for depth, short enough to sustain a single controlling idea. Tolstoy needed 580,000 words for War and Peace because his subject required that scale; most long novels are not Tolstoy.</div>
      </div>



      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">8. Is Shakespeare really better than every other playwright, or is that just cultural prestige?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Cultural prestige is real, but Shakespeare's technical superiority is not manufactured. The range is unmatched: comedy, tragedy, history, and romance, each executed at the highest level. The language works simultaneously at three registers — the plot level, the character psychology level, and the metaphoric-thematic level — in a way no other playwright has sustained across a whole body of work. Harold Bloom's argument in The Western Canon (1994) that Shakespeare invented the "human" as we now understand it — the self-reflecting, internally contradictory individual — is overstated but not entirely wrong. That said, Sophocles, Chekhov, and Beckett each achieve things Shakespeare doesn't attempt, and comparison across eras is partly a category error.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">9. What does Chekhov's gun principle actually mean, and does it apply to drama?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Chekhov's principle — if a gun appears on stage in Act 1, it must fire by Act 3 — is a principle of economy and expectation management, not a rule about firearms. In a play, stage space is limited and every object placed within it carries significance by default. If you draw attention to something and then don't use it, you create frustration; if you draw attention to something and then do use it, you create inevitability. Interestingly, Chekhov's own plays violate the spirit of this rule constantly — his plots are famously inconclusive, filled with loaded moments that don't discharge. This was deliberate: his drama is about the gap between what people feel and what they can act on.</div>
      </div>


      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">10. Is postmodernism just nihilism with footnotes?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Postmodernism is not nihilism — though some practitioners made it look that way. The core claim is epistemological, not ethical: that all narratives are constructed, that "grand narratives" (progress, enlightenment, redemption) are contingent cultural stories rather than universal truths. This is a philosophically serious position. What postmodern literature does well is expose the machinery of narrative — the way convention naturalises itself. What it does badly is substitute cleverness for meaning. The best postmodern writers (Pynchon, DeLillo, Borges) use self-consciousness to say something true about the world; the worst use it to avoid saying anything at all.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">11. Was Romanticism just adolescent sentimentality about nature?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">This is the standard charge — and it ignores what Romanticism was actually arguing. Romanticism emerged in the 1780s as a precise intellectual response to Enlightenment rationalism. Its claim was not that nature is pretty but that the reduction of experience to measurable, calculable fact impoverished human life. Blake's attack on "dark Satanic mills" and "single vision and Newton's sleep" was a philosophical argument about what is lost when reason becomes the only mode of knowing. Wordsworth's "spots of time" theory in The Prelude (1805) anticipates modern neuroscience on autobiographical memory. Shelley's Defence of Poetry (1821) argues that poetry is the basis of moral imagination — a serious epistemological claim, not a nature walk.</div>
      </div>


      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">12. Should we stop teaching Shakespeare because he's not diverse enough?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">The question frames a false binary. Shakespeare's plays can be taught alongside Toni Morrison, Chinua Achebe, and Aphra Behn — the curriculum is not zero-sum. Removing Shakespeare because of his race and era would be a category error: we'd be judging a 16th-century writer by 21st-century political criteria that are irrelevant to his plays' literary achievement. The more interesting question is what happens when you add writers who were systematically excluded — how does Middlemarch look different after reading Ama Ata Aidoo? The canon enriches itself through expansion, not replacement.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">13. Is the concept of "great literature" itself a form of cultural imperialism?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">There is a real critique here: the criteria for literary greatness were largely developed by European academic institutions in a period of colonial dominance, and those criteria were then used to evaluate and dismiss non-Western literary traditions. But the conclusion that quality is therefore relative and the canon should be abandoned does not follow. The alternative is not relativism but better criteria — criteria capable of recognising the formal sophistication of the Mahabharata, the structural complexity of classical Chinese poetry, or the narrative innovation of African oral tradition. Literary quality is real; the question is whether our instruments for measuring it are adequate to the range of human literary achievement.</div>
      </div>



      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">14. Do you need to understand a poem fully to appreciate it?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">No — and insisting on full comprehension before permission to enjoy is one of the most common ways people stop themselves from reading poetry. T.S. Eliot wrote that poetry can communicate before it is understood; he meant that the sound, rhythm, and image can create an experience that precedes, and sometimes exceeds, conceptual understanding. Keats described negative capability — the ability to remain in uncertainty and doubt without an irritable reaching after fact — as a precondition for great poetry. The same applies to reading it. Let meaning arrive gradually, over multiple readings and years.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">15. Is it possible to read too much criticism and lose your own response to a text?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Yes — and the best critics acknowledge this. Reading Hamlet before reading 50 years of Hamlet criticism gives you a response that is genuinely yours; reading the criticism first means you experience the play through other people's frameworks. The ideal sequence is read first, respond, then consult criticism to refine and challenge your reading. Criticism should amplify your engagement, not replace it. The danger of academic literary training is precisely that it substitutes the approved readings for personal response — which is why the best literary teachers always send students back to the text, not the criticism.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">16. How should I choose what to read if I'm starting from scratch?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Start with what you already like, then let each book lead you to the next. If you enjoyed Crime and Punishment, read Notes from Underground; if you loved it, read The Brothers Karamazov. Genre fiction is a perfectly legitimate entry point: the best crime fiction (Chandler, Highsmith, le Carre) is literary fiction by any serious standard. The error is to begin with what you "ought" to read rather than what you want to read — obligation kills the reading habit faster than anything else. Difficulty should be chosen, not assigned. When you're ready for Middlemarch, you'll know; and when you get there, you'll find it isn't difficult at all.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">17. Do you need to know Greek mythology to understand Western literature?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Not to enjoy it, but yes to understand it fully. Greek mythology is the deep grammar of Western literary allusion. When Keats writes "On First Looking into Chapman's Homer," the force of the poem depends on knowing what Homer meant to the ancient world. When Joyce names his novel Ulysses, the entire book is in dialogue with Homer's Odyssey. When a character is described as Promethean, Oedipal, or Narcissistic, those words carry myth inside them. You can experience the surface without the mythology; the depth is inaccessible without it. A few weeks with a good mythology handbook (Graves, Edith Hamilton, or Ovid's Metamorphoses directly) pays compound literary interest for a lifetime.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <div class="qa-q">18. Are myths just ancient religious belief, or do they have something to say to modern readers?</div>
        <button class="qa-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show'); this.textContent = this.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('show') ? 'hide' : 'show answer'">show answer</button>
        <div class="qa-ans">Myths are not failed or superseded religion. They are narrative patterns that encode persistent aspects of human experience. The Oedipus myth is interesting not because ancient Greeks believed it literally, but because Freud found in it a map of the unconscious that still generates clinical and interpretive work. The myth of Persephone still structures how we think about seasons, loss, and return. The story of Prometheus still describes the ambivalence we feel about technology and human overreach. Myths survive because the problems they encode — how to live with mortality, what we owe each other, how power corrupts — are not problems that get solved. They get re-staged in new form in every generation.</div>
      </div>



  

  
  
