      forms: 'Primary epics (oral, anonymous, Homeric): the Iliad, Odyssey, Gilgamesh, Beowulf, the Mahabharata. Literary (secondary) epics (composed in writing by a named poet): Virgil\'s Aeneid (Roman founding myth), Dante\'s Commedia, Milton\'s Paradise Lost. Epic conventions: invocation of the Muse, in medias res beginning, catalogue of warriors, epic simile, divine machinery.',
      challenge: 'Reading an epic requires patience with an entirely different concept of narrative time. Nothing happens quickly. The epic digresses, catalogues, repeats, describes at length. This is not a failure of technique; it is a different conception of what narrative is for — establishing the world fully before moving through it.',
