    { icon: '5-7-5', label: 'Haiku', sub: 'Japanese, 17 syllables', detail: 'The haiku is 3 lines of 5, 7, and 5 syllables — but syllable count is the wrong way to think about it. The haiku is two images in juxtaposition, with a cut (kireji) between them. Matsuo Basho (1644–1694) established the form: "An old silent pond / A frog jumps into the pond / Splash! Silence again." The juxtaposition creates meaning by implication. Haiku is an art of what is not said — what the reader\'s mind supplies in the gap between the two images.' },
    { icon: '||', label: 'Free verse', sub: 'No fixed metre', detail: 'Free verse abandons fixed metre and rhyme scheme but is not the absence of form. Walt Whitman\'s Leaves of Grass (1855) established the American free verse tradition: long, sweeping lines, catalogues, apostrophes, the rhythms of the speaking voice. T.S. Eliot said that "no verse is free for the man who wants to do a good job." Free verse creates rhythm through repetition, anaphora (starting lines the same way), caesura (a mid-line pause), and the line break itself. The constraint is self-imposed rather than inherited — which makes it harder, not easier.' },
    { icon: 'ab', label: 'Terza Rima', sub: 'Interlocking rhyme', detail: 'Terza rima is a three-line stanza form with interlocking rhyme: ABA BCB CDC... invented by Dante for the Divina Commedia. The forward-pulling rhyme scheme (each stanza rhymes with the next, creating a chain) is perfectly suited to a journey narrative. Shelley used it in Ode to the West Wind. The form creates momentum — you cannot stop because the rhyme scheme always leaves one line unresolved, pulling you into the next stanza.' },
