  const narrElements = [
    { icon: '👁', label: 'Point of view', sub: 'Who narrates', detail: 'Point of view (POV) determines what we can know and how we know it. First person (I) creates intimacy and limitation — the narrator can only tell us what they experienced or were told. Third person limited (she/he) stays close to one character\'s perspective; third person omniscient moves freely between all minds. Second person (you) is rare and creates uncanny effects (used by Italo Calvino in If on a winter\'s night a traveler). The choice of POV is not technical — it is an argument about knowledge: who is authorised to tell this story, and what can they know?' },
    { icon: '⏱', label: 'Time', sub: 'When and how', detail: 'Novelists manipulate time in four basic ways: summary (passing over long periods quickly), scene (dramatising a moment in detail), pause (stopping the story for description or digression), and ellipsis (skipping over periods entirely). Anachrony — telling events out of chronological order — creates meaning through juxtaposition: what does it mean that we know the ending before we understand how it happened? Proust\'s In Search of Lost Time is largely about the difference between lived time (discontinuous, affective) and clock time (linear, mechanical).' },
    { icon: '🎭', label: 'Character', sub: 'Who inhabits the world', detail: '"Round" characters (E.M. Forster\'s term) are capable of surprising us convincingly — they feel three-dimensional. "Flat" characters are defined by a single trait or function. The greatest novels are full of characters who feel as real as anyone we know: Emma Woodhouse, Anna Karenina, Raskolnikov, Isabel Archer, Atticus Finch. What makes them feel real is not physical description but the consistency of their interior logic — they always act from recognisable motives, even when those motives surprise us.' },
    { icon: '🗺', label: 'Setting', sub: 'Where the world is', detail: 'Setting is not merely background. In Wuthering Heights, the Yorkshire moors are as much a character as Catherine or Heathcliff. In García Márquez\'s Macondo, the town embodies an entire history of colonialism, magic, and isolation. The most powerful settings create what Henry James called "the air of reality" — a density of physical and social detail that makes the world feel inhabited rather than invented. Setting also controls temperature and pace: outdoor scenes in novels often accelerate; domestic interior scenes slow the narrative down.' },
    { icon: '💬', label: 'Dialogue', sub: 'Characters speaking', detail: 'Good fictional dialogue is not transcription of real speech — it is a stylised version that feels real while doing more work. Every line of dialogue should simultaneously reveal character, advance plot, create tension, and sound natural. Elmore Leonard\'s maxim: if it sounds like writing, cut it. The hardest dialogue to write is exposition — conveying information the characters already know through their speech. Ibsen mastered retrospective exposition (revealing the past through present dialogue); Harold Pinter mastered what is not said, what is implied, the pause as communication.' },
    { icon: '🌡', label: 'Tone', sub: 'The narrator\'s attitude', detail: 'Tone is the narrator\'s or implied author\'s emotional attitude toward the material. Austen\'s tone is ironic: she says one thing and means another, and the gap between them is where her comedy lives. Dostoevsky\'s tone is feverish, urgent, almost unbearably intense. Chekhov\'s is compassionate and exact. Toni Morrison\'s is magisterial. Tone is established through diction (word choice), sentence rhythm, what is emphasised and what is passed over, and the distance between narrator and character. A novel\'s tone is its most intimate quality — the register at which it addresses you.' },
